EU Data Hosting: We’re moving forwardBy: Nick O'Donovan ChatGPTClaudePerplexityGoogle AIFor some organizations, EU data hosting is a requirement before they can choose a security partner. For others, it may not be required, but is strongly desired. Huntress has gained a significant number of partners and customers in Europe over the last three years, and we're incredibly grateful for their feedback on data hosting and how we can tailor our platform for their unique needs.We're now actively working toward EU data hosting. But getting there will take time. It requires significant changes to how our platform is built and operated, so we'll move in phases and share more as the work progresses. Our official EU Data Hosting statement lays out our official plans and serves as the source of truth as they evolve, but this post will cover the basics.A first step: ingesting product data in the EUWe've already kicked off our EU data hosting initiative, and we're halfway through Phase 1, which will continue through the end of 2026. Here's what that means practically: for newly created accounts that select EU hosting, raw product data collected by supported Huntress products, along with backups, will be ingested and stored in a data center in Frankfurt, Germany. The products in scope are EDR, ESPM, SIEM, ITDR for Microsoft 365, and ISPM for Microsoft 365.It's important to understand what this first step does and doesn't mean. Phase 1 brings ingestion and storage of raw product data into the EU, but not all processing. Huntress detection and investigation systems will continue to operate in the United States. Data needed for analysis—personal data such as usernames, email addresses, IP addresses, and hostnames—will be transferred to the US for processing. The results of that analysis—including detections (alerts), incident reports, and forensic evidence—will also remain in the US, along with account and billing data.Phase 1 plan for Huntress EU data hostingEU hosting will be selected at the account level, so all organizations within an account will use the same region. Existing customers who want EU hosting will need to create a second account. Data already stored in the US won't be moved to the EU during Phase 1.The bottom line is that Phase 1 brings raw product data and backups into EU storage, while detection and investigation processing and their results remain in the US. We understand this still isn't ideal, which is why we're not stopping here.What comes nextIn Phase 2, we plan to move more of the work itself into the EU. Telemetry and forensic data, along with analysis results such as detections and investigations, would be processed and stored there. Some information would still remain in the US, including certain identifiers used to track and manage detections, as well as account, billing, and contact information. We're also evaluating options for migrating existing customers' historical data.Phase 2 plan for Huntress EU data hostingAfter Phase 2, we'll assess whether even more customer product and personal data should be brought into the EU. That longer-term work is less certain, and we'll define it as we make progress. We aren't committing to timelines for Phase 2 or beyond yet. We'll share more as plans develop.It's important to note that Huntress will continue to operate a global Security Operations Center (SOC). Authorized team members outside the EU may access EU-hosted data when needed to support customers, respond to incidents, and help maintain the security and availability of our services. Interested in testing?If you're a partner or customer interested in taking part in Phase 1 testing, let your Huntress account manager know. We welcome your feedback as we work through this in stages.For the complete scope and latest details, please refer to the official EU Data Hosting statement (if anything in this post differs from that statement, the statement takes precedence). You might also […]

